## Architecture

- Layer discipline: Controller → Service → Repository → Model
- 12-factor app compliance
- Domain exceptions mapped to HTTP at `GlobalExceptionHandler` boundary
- All endpoints authenticated unless explicitly marked `.permitAll()` in `SecurityFilterChain`
- DTOs at every API boundary — entities never leave the service layer
- Constructor injection only — never `@Autowired` on fields

## Non-Negotiable Rules

1. `google-java-format` clean — 2-space indent, never tabs.
2. Javadoc on all public service interface methods, starting with an imperative verb.
3. No hardcoded secrets — `@ConfigurationProperties` typed records loaded from env vars.
4. `@Slf4j` + SLF4J `{}` placeholders — never `System.out.println` or `java.util.logging`.
5. Spring Data derived queries or JPQL named parameters — no concatenated SQL.
6. Tests mandatory — 80% instruction coverage minimum (JaCoCo enforced in CI).
7. Always throw typed `AppException` subclasses — never catch and swallow silently.
8. `@Valid` on every `@RequestBody` parameter — without exception.
9. Monetary values: `new BigDecimal("19.99")` from String — never `new BigDecimal(19.99)`.
10. `@Transactional` on public methods only — private methods silently ignored by Spring AOP.
11. `ResponseEntity<T>` return type on all controller methods — no raw types.
12. Flyway for all schema changes — never `ddl-auto=update` in non-local environments.

## Code Style

- `google-java-format` (enforced by CI and pre-commit hook)
- Checkstyle + SpotBugs + PMD with project `pom.xml` config
- Import order: static → java.* → third-party → com.nmn.*
- 2-space indent, 120-character line limit
- Records for immutable DTOs and response objects
- Sealed classes for closed type hierarchies
- camelCase methods/variables, PascalCase classes, UPPER_SNAKE_CASE constants

